From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: DTLS 1.2 AES-128-GCM records sealed by the transport, a burst's together

OpenSSL seals each application record on its own: one request to the crypto engine, GHASH on the CPU and its record
layer around them, about a quarter of a data channel's CPU at full rate. With a DtlsRecordSealer installed
(rtc::SetDtlsRecordSealer), a DTLS 1.2 connection that negotiated ECDHE-ECDSA- or ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256
derives its write key and salt from the session (RFC 5246 key expansion) and proves them: one of OpenSSL's own
application records must open with them, or OpenSSL keeps every record. Once the peer's first application record
has arrived, so OpenSSL no longer resends its Finished, the transport builds the records itself, numbering on from
the last one OpenSSL wrote; the takeover happens under the SSL lock, so no SSL_write is under way. A burst's records
(BurstScope) are numbered, sealed by the sealer in one call and handed down under one lock, so their order on the
wire is the order of their numbers; a record outside a burst goes the same way alone. Records the sealer does not
take are sealed with EVP. Renegotiation is off already (SSL_OP_NO_RENEGOTIATION). After the takeover OpenSSL's own
records in that epoch (its close_notify) are not sent: their sequence numbers are taken. The nonces cannot meet
OpenSSL's either: its explicit nonce starts at a random 64-bit value (gcm_tls_iv_set_fixed), these are epoch and
sequence number. Other suites, DTLS versions and TLS libraries are unchanged.
